  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure is beginning to build into much of the Midwest and\nGreat Lakes regions today. The low pressure that provided cool and\ncloudy conditions this weekend continues to pull away to the east. \nHowever, as the weather pattern changes, the transition period can \nprovide a few forecast challenges. \n\nFor Monday, how quickly the clouds clear across the entire area is\nthe biggest challenge. Lingering moisture trapped within the high\npressure takes time to mix out throughout the morning and afternoon\nunder calm winds. Some areas may also see sunshine through much of \nthe day. After coordination with surrounding offices, cloud cover is \nnow forecast to linger into the afternoon, which is a few hours later\nthan the NBM. The higher chances for cloud cover to linger is across\nwest-central and central Ohio. Confidence is a bit higher that the \nentire area will be mostly clear by the evening.\n\nClear skies Monday night result in the second forecast challenge\nduring the pattern transition. Strong high pressure over the region\nwill provide calm winds and clear skies through Tuesday. This will\nfavor the potential for fog development across much of the area\nMonday night into Tuesday morning. Areas of dense fog should be\nexpected in favorable locations outside the major metropolitan areas. \n\nOtherwise, the high pressure remains in place through the end of the\nweek, providing gradually warming conditions above seasonal normals.",
